# ===========================================================================
# PUBLIC — safe to ship to the browser (Next.js exposes anything prefixed
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ client-side). Never put a real secret behind this prefix.
# ===========================================================================

# Supabase project — used for Auth (login/register/session) and Storage
# (private per-user document/lecture-slide uploads). The anon/publishable
# key is NOT secret (Supabase designs it to be shipped to the browser); it
# only works within whatever your RLS policies allow.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="sb_publishable_..."

# ===========================================================================
# SERVER-ONLY SECRETS — never prefix these with NEXT_PUBLIC_, never send
# them to the browser. In Vercel, add these under Project Settings →
# Environment Variables (they're server-only there by default — only
# NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed vars ever reach client code).
#
# Both database URLs below embed the database password, so they belong in
# this section and not the public one above.
# ===========================================================================

# The connection the running app uses for every query. Copy it from the
# Supabase dashboard → Connect → "Transaction pooler"; do not hand-assemble
# the host, since the region and pooler generation differ per project.
#
# Two things about it matter for performance, and both are easy to get
# wrong:
#
#  * It must be the pooler host on port 6543, not the direct
#    db.<project-ref>.supabase.co host on 5432. Serverless functions start
#    cold constantly, and against the direct host every cold start pays a
#    fresh TCP handshake, TLS handshake and Postgres startup before the
#    first row is read. The direct host is also IPv6-only and unreachable
#    from most serverless platforms, Vercel included.
#
#  * `pgbouncer=true` is required. The transaction pooler hands a different
#    backend connection to each statement, so Prisma must stop relying on
#    server-side prepared statements. Omitting this produces intermittent
#    "prepared statement already exists" errors under concurrency.
#
# connection_limit sizes Prisma's own client-side pool, per function
# instance. Do NOT set this to 1 here.
#
# The "use connection_limit=1 in serverless" advice is about connecting
# straight to Postgres, where each client connection is a real backend
# process and the database's max_connections is the scarce resource. Behind
# the transaction pooler that is no longer the shape of the problem:
# Supavisor multiplexes many client connections onto few real backends, so
# holding several is cheap.
#
# What is expensive here is serialising. The dashboard alone fans out to
# roughly 25 queries through nested Promise.all (the priority engine, the
# academic-health score and the dashboard's own batch). With a limit of 1
# they queue behind a single connection, and if the wait ever exceeds
# Prisma's pool_timeout (10s by default) they fail with P2024.
#
# 10 is a sane value for this app: comfortably above the widest fan-out
# without being an unreasonable number of client slots to hold. Supavisor
# logs confirm it copes with the full burst — a working deploy shows a
# dozen-plus "Connection authenticated" lines inside the same second.
#
# THE MOST COMMON MISTAKE HERE IS NOT THE POOL SIZE, IT IS THE PASSWORD.
# The dashboard's connection string is shown with the password replaced by
# a "[YOUR-PASSWORD]" placeholder, and pasting it unedited (or with the
# wrong password) fails in a way that looks nothing like an auth problem
# from the outside: the deploy builds fine, the site loads, sign-in works
# because that goes through Supabase Auth rather than Postgres, and only
# the pages that read the database break. Supavisor logs the truth as
# `ClientHandler: Exchange error: password authentication failed`, visible
# under Logs → Pooler in the Supabase dashboard. If the password contains
# any of @ : / ? # [ ] % or a space, percent-encode it.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@<pooler-host>:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=10"

# Used ONLY by Prisma's schema tooling (`prisma migrate`, `prisma db pull`,
# `prisma studio`) — never by the running app, and never on a request path.
# Those commands need a session-mode connection, which the transaction
# pooler above cannot provide.
#
# Copy the Supabase dashboard's "Session pooler" string (the pooler host on
# port 5432) rather than the direct connection: it is session-mode as
# required, and unlike the direct host it is reachable over IPv4, so it also
# works from CI and from machines without IPv6. Use the direct
# db.<project-ref>.supabase.co:5432 string instead only if you know your
# environment has IPv6.
#
# Leaving this unset is fine if you apply schema changes through the
# Supabase SQL editor, which is what this project does — `prisma generate`
# and `next build` both succeed without it.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@<pooler-host>:5432/postgres"

# Required ONLY for the background file-processing job
# (src/app/api/cron/process-documents/route.ts). That job runs outside any
# user's session — triggered by Vercel Cron, not a logged-in request — so
# it can't use a per-user access token like the rest of the app; it needs
# the service role key, which bypasses Storage RLS by design. Get it from
# Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role.
# Without it, uploaded documents stay QUEUED (never processed, never
# incorrectly marked FAILED) until this is set.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="eyJ..."

# Authenticates Vercel's own cron trigger against the processing route —
# NOT a Supabase credential. Vercel automatically sends this as
# "Authorization: Bearer <value>" on every request it makes to a
# vercel.json cron path when this env var is set; the route rejects any
# request whose bearer token doesn't match. Generate any long random
# string (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`) and set the same value in Vercel.
CRON_SECRET="a-long-random-string"

# OPTIONAL — turns on the inbox's automatic sorting.
#
# When this is set, items dropped into /inbox are read by a real model,
# which proposes what each one is, which of YOUR subjects it belongs to,
# and where it could be filed. Nothing it proposes is ever saved on its
# own: every proposal waits for you to confirm or change it.
#
# When it is NOT set, the app does not pretend to have analysed anything.
# Captures are stored exactly as dropped and marked UNPROCESSED, the inbox
# says plainly that automatic sorting is off, and you file items yourself.
# That is a fully working state, not a degraded one — leave this unset if
# you would rather not send document text to a third party.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."

# Optional override for the model the agent runs on.
#
# The default is claude-opus-5, NOT sonnet — this file said sonnet for months
# and it was wrong, which understated the bill by about five times. Opus is the
# default on purpose: organising a drop is judgement work (is this a marked exam
# to log as a mistake, or a lecture to file?), and a wrong decision writes a
# wrong row into a student's real records. Set this to claude-sonnet-5 if you
# would rather pay less and accept more mistakes.
AI_MODEL="claude-opus-5"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Spending limits (all optional, all defaulted in code — see
# src/lib/ai/agent/budget.ts)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every drop calls a model, and the bill goes to whoever's key is above.
# Registration is open, so the number of drops is not bounded by anything except
# these. Leaving all three unset is SAFE: each one falls back to the default
# named beside it. So does a typo — a zero, a negative or a word lands on the
# default rather than on "no limit" or "refuse everything".
#
# One run. Bounded already by the loop's 8 steps, so this only trims a
# pathological run; the largest legitimate one measured by arithmetic (a
# hundred-page PDF re-read across eight cached steps) is about $2.60.
# Default: 3
# AI_SPEND_CAP_USD="3"

# One account, rolling 24 hours. About a twelve-pass textbook plus a normal
# day's lecture drops.
# Default: 5
# AI_DAY_CAP_USD="5"

# EVERY account together, rolling 24 hours. This is the one that answers the
# real exposure: the per-account limit is only as strong as the number of
# accounts somebody is willing to create. A student may never be allowed more
# than this, so setting it below AI_DAY_CAP_USD lowers both.
# Default: 10
# AI_DAY_CAP_TOTAL_USD="10"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transcription (optional) — turns a recorded lecture into words
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The model above reads text, images and PDFs. It does not listen to sound, so
# a recorded lecture needs a second provider — and therefore a second key and a
# second bill, which is a decision only whoever pays for it can make.
#
# Unset, the app is honest about it: a recording is stored, the inbox says
# nobody listened to it, and nothing pretends otherwise. That is a working
# state, not a broken one.
#
# Set, a recorded lecture becomes a transcript the agent organises like any
# other material — which is the single largest thing this app can do that a
# student cannot do for themselves.
#
# Any OpenAI-compatible transcription endpoint works; only the base URL and the
# model name differ between them.
# TRANSCRIPTION_API_KEY="sk-..."
# TRANSCRIPTION_BASE_URL="https://api.openai.com/v1"
# TRANSCRIPTION_MODEL="whisper-1"

# Optional. Free YouTube Data API v3 key (Google Cloud console).
# Turns on in-app results for "find a clip for this procedure". Without it
# the feature hands over a YouTube search URL instead — it does not break.
YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# prompts.chat — a developer tool, not part of the app
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Read by .mcp.json, which wires prompts.chat into Claude Code as an MCP
# server. Nothing in src/ touches it: the running app neither sends this key
# nor needs it, and a deployment without it is in no way degraded.
#
# IT IS A REFERENCE IN .mcp.json, NOT A VALUE. `.mcp.json` lives in the repo
# root and is NOT covered by .gitignore, so a key written into it is a key
# committed to the repository and then present in every clone and every line of
# history. Claude Code expands ${VAR} in an http server's url and headers,
# which is what keeps the secret out of the file.
#
# THIS VARIABLE IS NOT READ FROM .env.local, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE REASON
# THIS PARAGRAPH EXISTS. An earlier version of this entry said it was, which
# was wrong and tested wrong on 2026-10-08: with PROMPTS_API_KEY in .env.local,
# `claude mcp list` still reported "Missing environment variables"; with the
# same value exported in the shell, the warning cleared. `.env.local` is a
# Next.js convention that the Next build reads — it is not the environment
# Claude Code starts in, and nothing in src/ reads this key anyway.
#
# So it belongs in the SHELL Claude Code is launched from:
#
#   export PROMPTS_API_KEY="pchat_..."     # in ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc
#
# or in `.claude/settings.local.json` under its "env" key, which is gitignored
# for exactly this purpose. Either way the value never enters the repository.
#
# It is listed here, among keys the app does read, only so that nobody goes
# looking for it in src/ and concludes it is missing.
PROMPTS_API_KEY=
